sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch13-21> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The leading facts of embryology are explained because, in the descendants of a common progenitor, successive slight modifications generally do not appear at a very early period of life (though they may be caused earlier) and are inherited at a corresponding later age, leaving embryos little modified.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Embryological facts follow from late-appearing variations inherited at corresponding ages";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1st ed., London: John Murray, 1859), Chapter XIII, “Mutual Affinities of Organic Beings: Morphology: Embryology: Rudimentary Organs”; section “EMBRYOLOGY, laws of, explained by variations not supervening at an early age, and being inherited at a corresponding age”. Scope: animals generally"
}
